g 49.50. At the US Olympic Trials Dressel recorded the third fastest time ever with 49.76 and came into Tokyo with 7 of the top 10 times in the event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/2503191"} "Kristóf Milak"] ", who was considered better in the 200 fly, was the only swimmer close to Dressel on time but still had never broken 50 seconds. Another challenger was France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/133911"} "Mehdy Metella"] " but he had had shoulder injuries and would not make the final."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the prelims Dressel equalled " [:a {:href "/athletes/121581"} "Joseph Schooling"] "’s Olympic record from Rio, leading the way with 50.39. He then bettered that in the semi-finals with a time of 49.71 as Milak qualified second with 50.31. The final saw Dressel out the fastest, splitting 23.00 with Milak and ROC swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/2503916"} "Andrey Minakov"] " 0.65 seconds back at the turn. Dressel slowed slightly coming in but still broke his own world record with a time of 49.45 as Milak closed quickly to finish in a European record 49.68, making him the second fastest performer ever. Behind them Minakov could not hold on as Swiss swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/2504016"} "Noe Ponti"] " took bronze."] "\n\n" [:p {} "For Dressel this was his second individual gold at Tokyo. He would later win the 50 freestyle, and help the United States win the men’s 4 x 100 free relay and the mixed medley relay to leave Tokyo with five gold medals."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/136353"} "Caeleb Dressel"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "50.39 (1 h8)"] [:td {} "49.